      "costUsd": 0.005926000000000001,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's the city's daylight over one year, and the line at thirteen hours. Between zero and two pi, they cross twice, once in late October and once in late February. A calculator's inverse sine gives you only one angle, so where does the second day come from, and how do we know there isn't a third? The graph answers both. Every solution is a crossing, and every crossing must sit inside the stated domain, here one year."
    },
