      "inputTokens": 157,
      "outputTokens": 1185,
      "costUsd": 0.0107435,
      "pronunciationText": "A number multiplying x packs more cycles into the domain, like two years of daylight on one graph. Solve two sine two x equals root three, for x from zero to two pi. Divide by two: sine two x is root three over two. Let u equal two x. As x runs from zero to two pi, u runs from zero to four pi. That's two full cycles, so expect four crossings. In the first cycle, u is pi over three and two pi over three. Add two pi for the second cycle: seven pi over three and eight pi over three. Halve them all: x is pi over six, pi over three, seven pi over six, and four pi over three. Stop at two, and you lose half the answers."
    },
